{title: Be Thou My Vision}
{lyricist: Irish, 8th c.; tr. Mary E. Byrne, 1905; versified Eleanor H. Hull, 1912}
{composer: SLANE, Irish folk melody}
{copyright: Public domain}
{key: D}
{time: 3/4}
{tempo: 84}

{start_of_verse}
[D]Be Thou my [G]Vi[D]sion, O [G]Lord of my [A]heart;
[G]Naught be all [D]else to me, [G]save that Thou [A]art;
[D]Thou my best [G]Thought, by [A]day or by [Bm]night,
[G]Waking or [D]sleeping, Thy [A]presence my [D]light.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
[D]Be Thou my [G]Wis[D]dom, and [G]Thou my true [A]Word;
[G]I ever [D]with Thee and [G]Thou with me, [A]Lord;
[D]Thou my great [G]Father, [A]I Thy true [Bm]son;
[G]Thou in me [D]dwelling, and [A]I with Thee [D]one.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
[D]Riches I [G]heed [D]not, nor [G]man's empty [A]praise,
[G]Thou mine In[D]heritance, [G]now and al[A]ways:
[D]Thou and Thou [G]only, [A]first in my [Bm]heart,
[G]High King of [D]Heaven, my [A]Treasure Thou [D]art.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
[D]High King of [G]Hea[D]ven, my [G]victory [A]won,
[G]May I reach [D]Heaven's joys, O [G]bright Heaven's [A]Sun!
[D]Heart of my [G]own heart, [A]whatever be[Bm]fall,
[G]Still be my [D]Vision, O [A]Ruler of [D]all.
{end_of_verse}
